{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-160419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\pi .$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n- $y=\\pi $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi ,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y+\\pi .$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-0+\\pi =\\pi \\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi =1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=\\pi $ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\pi +\\pi =0-\\pi +\\pi =0.$$\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-160419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+y={y}^{2}-2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=2$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=4$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{2}-y-2,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{2}-2-2=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{2}-1-2=-2\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=4$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(4)={4}^{2}-4-2=10\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-96070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{e}^{2}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=1$\n- $y=e$\n- $y=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{e}^{2},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{e}^{2}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=(1)^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡1-{e}^{2}=-{e}^{2}\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=e$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(e)={e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-{e}^{2}={e}^{2}-{e}^{2}=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-{e}^{2}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-96072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}=1.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-1$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)=1-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1)=1-\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)=1-\\frac{1}{{1}^{2}}=1-1=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)=1-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{4}\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160422" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-160422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160422" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{2}=2y+{y}^{3}.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=0$\n- $y=1$\n- $y=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y)={y}^{3}+{y}^{2}+2y.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=0$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(0)={0}^{3}+{0}^{2}+2(0)=0.$$\\,✓$\n\n- $y=1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(1)={1}^{3}+{1}^{2}+2(1)=4\\ne 0.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- $y=2$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(2)={2}^{3}+{2}^{2}+2(2)=16\\ne 0.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95572" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-95572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95572" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-6y+9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-6y+9,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-6c+9 & =0 \\\\ (c-3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ c-3 & =0 \\\\ c & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{1}(x)=3$  is the only equilibrium solution of the given differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96159" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-96159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=-6$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=9$"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=1$"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ only"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=3$ only"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=6$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96159" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "${y}_{1}(x)=3$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2,$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c+2 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡c & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡c|\\le 1$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160429" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-160429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y(x)=0$ only"] ["text" "$y(x)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\pi $ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=0$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\pi $"] ["text" "The equation has no equilibrium solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160429" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The equation has no equilibrium solutions"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-352884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "**In ascending order**, the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{2}-2y-15$\nare\n\n${y}_{1}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}(t)=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-352884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{2}-2y-15,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}-2c-15 & =0 \\\\ (c-5)(c+3) & =0 \\\\ c & =-3,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, ${y}_{1}(t)=-3$ and ${y}_{2}(t)=5$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-352884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-352884" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-352884" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-352884" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/De