e" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-352876" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-352876" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{4}+2},$ so we attempt solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{c}^{4}+2} & =0 \\\\ {c}^{4}+2 & =0 \\\\ {c}^{4} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, note that ${c}^{4}\\ge 0$ for all $c.$ Therefore, the above equation has no (real) solutions.\n\nTherefore, the differential equation has no equilibrium points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160432" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-160432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$y(x)=-\\sqrt[4]{2}$ only"] ["text" "$y(x)=\\sqrt[4]{2}$ only"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt[4]{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt[4]{2}$"] ["text" "${y}_{1}(x)=-\\sqrt[4]{2},\\,{y}_{2}(x)=0,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=1$ and ${y}_{4}(x)=\\sqrt[4]{2}$"] ["text" "The equation has no equilibrium solutions"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160432" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The equation has no equilibrium solutions"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-7y+6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, ${y}_{1}(x),{y}_{2}(x),$ and ${y}_{3}(x)$\n- ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(x)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7y+6.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}-7(1)+6=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-1)({y}^{2}+y-6) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-2)(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(x)=2,\\,{y}_{3}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have ${y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)+{y}_{3}(x)=1+2+(-3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95733" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-95733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-95733" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y+8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=2$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+12y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-2)=(-2)^{3}+6(-2)^{2}+12(-2)+8=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(2)=(2)^{3}+6(2)^{2}+12(2)+8=64\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-2$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+2)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+2)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ | $8$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-2$ | $-8$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+2)({y}^{2}+4y+4) \\\\  & =(y+2)(y+2)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+2)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-96175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96175" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}-7{y}^{2}+15y-9.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is a equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}-7{y}^{2}+15y-9.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(3)=(3)^{3}-7(3)^{2}+15(3)-9=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false.  We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}-7(-1)^{2}+15(-1)-9=-32\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We know that $y=3$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y-3)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-3)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-7$ | $15$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $-12$ | $9$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-4$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-3)({y}^{2}-4y+3) \\\\  & =(y-3)(y-1)(y-3) \\\\  & =(y-1)(y-3)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{y}_{2}(t)=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-96174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96174" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y(t)=-1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y(t)=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+3y+1.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have that $f(-1)=(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+3(-1)+1=0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have that $f(1)={1}^{3}+3(1)^{2}+3(1)+1=8\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-1$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+1)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+1)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+1)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+1)(y+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(y+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has exactly one equilibrium solution,\n\n$$\ny(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-160444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-160444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+9y+4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- $y=-4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation ${y}^{′}(t)=f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)={y}^{3}+6{y}^{2}+9y+4.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is false.  We have that $f(1)=(1)^{3}+6(1)^{2}+9(1)+4=20\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  We have that $f(-4)=(-4)^{3}+6(-4)^{2}+9(-4)+4=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We know that $y=-4$ is a solution to $f(y)=0,$ and so $(y+4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y+4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | ${y}^{3}$ | ${y}^{2}$ | ${y}^{1}$ | ${y}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-4$ | $-8$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y+4)({y}^{2}+2y+1) \\\\  & =(y+4)(y+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has $2$ distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(t)=-4,\\,{y}_{2}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96176" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "460a86ba02198f39f4997b9cc2ccc880e77af283379a798a918c9114fe0d141f"} {"question" "q-96176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "q-96176" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/state.json" "file_sha256" "b6c1e1e688900022f3c64df77ec60a04f5d73eb785e1be7bb980f97f7b13ff27"} {"question" "e-8409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} + y + 6 = y^2.$ Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n- $y=-3$\n\n- $y=-2$\n\n- $y=-1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/example-8409.json" "file_sha256" "716d29338a7fc3cea18328ecdc3860ca6015069e2a6f145da9c4a4fb4387509b"} {"question" "e-8409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(t) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d t} = y^2 - y - 6,\n$$\n\nand we get that $f(y) = y^2 - y - 6.$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y).$\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0.$  $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0.$$\\quad  \\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\n- $y= -1$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-1) = (-1)^2 - (-1) - 6 = -4 \\neq 0.$ $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/example-8409.json" "file_sha256" "716d29338a7fc3cea18328ecdc3860ca6015069e2a6f145da9c4a4fb4387509b"} {"question" "e-8410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^2+4y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/example-8410.json" "file_sha256" "3ddc3e534ff7b0db82ef10237979a1c01d4bb4be1e20cea3ce549c3fece37038"} {"question" "e-8410" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nSo, to find the equilibrium solutions of a differential equation, we must find all values of $c$ such that $f(c)=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)= y^2+4y,$ so we solve the equation $f(c)=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =0 \\\\ {c}^{2}+4c & =0 \\\\ c(c+4) & =0 \\\\ c & =-4,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_1(x)=-4$ and $y_2(x)=0$ are the equilibrium solutions of the given differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/example-8410.json" "file_sha256" "3ddc3e534ff7b0db82ef10237979a1c01d4bb4be1e20cea3ce549c3fece37038"} {"question" "e-8411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm  d x}= y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y_1(x)=4$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n\n- The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_1(x), y_2(x),$ and $y_3(x)$\n\n- $y_1(x) + y_2(x)+y_3(x) =-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/example-8411.json" "file_sha256" "ee08911dab1d9cf88746c0fe0c3c2c54e532c6eb1ce51f96cf12b6eb0478158a"} {"question" "e-8411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given an autonomous differential equation $y'(x) = f(y),$ if there exists a constant $c$ such that $f(c)=0,$ then $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nIn our case, we have that $f(y)=y^3-3y^2-6y+8.$\n\nLet's examine the statement one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true.  We have that $f(4)=4^3-3(4)^2-6(4)+8 = 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $y=4$ is a solution to $f(y) = 0,$ and so $(y-4)$ is a factor of the polynomial $f(y).$ Dividing $f(y)$ by $(y-4)$ using synthetic division, we get\n\n|  | $y^3$ | $y^2$ | $y^1$ | $y^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $1$ | $-3$ | $-6$ | $8$ |\n|  | $\\color{lightgray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $4$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\n \nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =(y-4)({y}^{2}+y-2) \\\\  & =(y-4)(y-1)(y+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(y)$ has three distinct equilibrium solutions, \n\n$$\ny_1(x) =4, \\quad y_2(x) = 1, \\quad y_3(x) = -2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have $y_1(x)+y_2(x) + y_3(x) = 4+1+(-2) = 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040030/example-8411.json" "file_sha256" "ee08911dab1d9cf88746c0fe0c3c2c54e532c6eb1ce51f96cf12b6eb0478158a"}] "usage" {"e-8410" [] "q-95733" [] "e-8411" [] "e-8409" [] "q-95572" []} "basis" 1435 "retractions" [[17592186167327 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} - y^{2} = 2y + y^{3}$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y = 0$\n2. $y = 1$\n3. $y = 2$"] [17592186167337 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{3} - 7y + 6$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = 1$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation\n2. The equation has $3$ distinct equilibrium solutions, $y_{1}(x), y_{2}(x)$, and $y_{3}(x)$\n3. $y_{1}(x) + y_{2}(x) + y_{3}(x) = 0$"] [17592186166558 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} + y + 6 = y^2$. Which of the following is an equilibrium solution of this equation?\n\n1. $y=-3$\n2. $y=-2$\n3. $y=-1$"] [17592186166558 :question/worked-solution "Given an autonomous differential equation\n\n$$\ny'(t) = f(y)\n$$\n\nif there exists a constant $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(c)=0\n$$\n\nthen $y=c$ is an equilibrium solution of the equation.\n\nFirst, we write the given equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = y^2 - y - 6\n$$\n\nand we get that\n\n$$\nf(y) = y^2 - y - 6\n$$\n\nNow, let's check each proposed solution by substituting them into $f(y)$.\n\n- $y=-3$ is *not* an equilibrium point. We have $f(-3) = (-3)^2 - (-3) - 6 = 6 \\neq 0$. $\\quad \\color{red}{\\times}$\n- $y = -2$ is an equilibrium point. We have $f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) - 6 = 0$.$\\quad \\color{green}